{
  "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
  "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Kannon K. Shanmugam (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHANMUGAM: Thank you, Mr. Chief\nJustice, and may it please the Court:\nThis case presents a question of\nstatutory interpretation involving the provision\nauthorizing appellate review of certain remand\norders. The relevant provision of\nSection 1447(d) authorizes appellate review of a\nremand order where a ground for removal was the\nfederal officer or civil rights removal statute.\nBy its plain terms, the statute\npermits review of the entire order, not\nparticular issues. The court of appeals'\ncontrary interpretation is invalid."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Thank you, Mr. Chief\nJustice. Mr. Shanmugam, the -- I'd like to -- I\nmay have missed your last point. Did you say\nthat even if the order or the bases offered by\nthe moving party is frivolous, that it would\nstill have to be considered?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: Well, one concern\nwould be that if you are right, that in\nconsidering removal, a defendant will add\ngrounds, federal officer or civil rights. There\nare -- there's a big difference between\nfrivolous and meritorious. It's called\nuncertainty and possible and who knows. So\nthey'll add, on those grounds, it will get\nover to the federal court. The federal court\nwill say that it -- it's not frivolous, but it's"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0001-0003",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "this Court, for more than a century, has applied federal common law to claims seeking redress for interstate pollution, including most notably in AEP with regard to very similar nuisance claims alleging injury from global climate change."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (amicus)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0001-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This Court should hold that Section 1447(d) means what it says. There is simply no natural way to read the phrase \"an order remanding a case\" as part of an order remanding a case. Therefore, once Section 1447(d) authorizes an appeal of a remand order, there's no basis for artificially limiting the scope of that appeal to a particular question."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "This case was not removed pursuant to 1442 because, as the courts below held, Petitioners do not qualify for federal officer removal."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 46,
        "start": 46
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Counsel, you just said that your -- the Petitioners' reading of 1447(d) is contrary to the language of the statute. I will give you an uninterrupted three minutes to explain to me how the language \"an order remanding a case shall be reviewable by appeal or otherwise\" should be read to say a portion of an order remanding a case shall be reviewable by appeal or otherwise, solely with respect to the text of that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioners assert that \"pursuant to\" is merely a label in the removal notice. But we know that can't be right because, for example, in an earlier section, in 1446, which is actually the procedure for removal of civil actions, the removing party has to --the attorney has to file pursuant to Rule 11."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0002-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE THOMAS: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Counsel, I -- I don't want to belabor the point, but I think it's an important point. Could you give us -- you mentioned some cases that support your point. Could you give us further details about those cases and why they support your point?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States amicus",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0002-0003",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "as the Court and the United States' amicus in that case said, that would be an -- an -- a very oblique way of attempting to overturn the settled body of law."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Kannon K. Shanmugam",
      "case_id": "2020-19-1189",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "ae28ba47a085c0207cea307a3d6e8fca5a69880230e8af03719b883d7a59e5ea",
      "evidence_id": "ev-ae28ba47a085-0003-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 75,
        "start": 75
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. SHANMUGAM: Thank you, Mr. Chief\nJustice.\nThere is one fundamental problem with\nRespondents' argument today. It finds no home\nin the actual language of Section 1447(d).\nThere's simply no way as a matter of\nordinary English to construe \"order\" to mean\nmerely a portion of an order. In Yamaha,\nJustice Ginsburg, writing for a unanimous court,\nrelied on that plain meaning to reach the same\nconclusion."
    },
